Done. All 15 assertions pass, no failures.

**`format.js`** — one new block at the end of the option chain:

```js
if (options.maxLength !== undefined && result.length > options.maxLength) {
  const ellipsis = "...";

  if (options.maxLength <= ellipsis.length) {
    result = ellipsis.slice(0, Math.max(0, options.maxLength));
  } else {
    const budget = result.slice(0, options.maxLength - ellipsis.length);
    const boundary = budget.search(/\s+\S*$/);
    const kept = boundary === -1 ? budget : budget.slice(0, boundary);
    result = kept + ellipsis;
  }
}
```

**`format.test.js`** — 10 new `console.assert` lines in the existing style: under/at the limit, word-boundary cut, no-boundary hard cut, whitespace at the cut, `maxLength` of 3, 2, and 0, and truncation after `prefix`/`suffix`.

Two notes on what changed against the design:

- The `trimEnd()` I described turned out to be unnecessary, so it isn't there. The boundary regex `\s+\S*$` matches the whole whitespace run, so cutting at its start already removes it — the `"abc   defgh"` → `"abc..."` test is what pins that down. I'd rather not carry a line the tests can't fail on.
- I added a `Math.max(0, ...)` guard and a `maxLength: 0` test. Without it a negative `maxLength` would hit `slice`'s negative-index behavior and return `".."` — longer than the limit, which is exactly the guarantee we picked. Not in the design; say the word if you'd rather it throw on bad input instead.

`node format.test.js` prints a pre-existing `MODULE_TYPELESS_PACKAGE_JSON` warning — the project has no `"type": "module"`. It predates this change and I left it alone; adding one line to `package.json` would silence it if you want that.

